# `M154g` step 2b / `C68`, `C69` — the known-answer plant for `expect` and `capture`, the two step
# keywords whose contracts are about **what does not happen next**.
#
# **This file is meant to fail**, in the same deliberate way `retry-attempt-budget.tflw` is: two of
# its three tests must end red, and a run in which they pass is the regression. It lives under
# `tests/.constructs/`, a dot-directory `tflw run` does not discover, so the suite stays green while
# the grader runs this file by name.
#
# ## Two contracts stated by `tflw spec --json`, neither of them ever observed here
#
#   `expect`   "hard assertion — … fails the test **immediately**"
#   `capture`  "a capture that resolves to nothing **fails the step** rather than binding `undefined`"
#
# Both are claims about a step that comes *after*, and that is exactly why 1692 uses of `expect` and
# 523 of `capture` cannot prove either. In the ordinary shape of a test the assertion is the last
# interesting thing that happens, so an `expect` that recorded its failure and carried on would look
# identical, and a `capture` that bound `undefined` would surface — if at all — as some later
# assertion failing for an unrelated-looking reason.
#
# `C1` is the other side of this pair and the reason it is worth stating as a number: `check` is
# specified to record a failure **and keep going**, and its plant proves the step after it ran. If
# `expect` and `check` were the same construct wearing two names, `C1` would still be green and this
# file would be the only thing that noticed.
#
# ## The known answer is a marked label that must be absent
#
# Each failing test marks `POST /v1/lifecycle/mark` immediately before the step that must fail, and
# again immediately after it. The claim is that the grader finds the *before* label and **not** the
# *after* one. An absence is a weak observation on its own, which is what the leading mark is for:
# `c68before` at 1 proves the test really ran and really reached the failing step, so `c68after`
# being missing is a fact about `expect` rather than about a test that never started.

before file
  api POST /lifecycle/reset
  expect status equals 200

@constructs @workhorses
test "a failing `expect` ends the test at once — the step after it never runs"
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c68before" }
  expect status equals 200

  # Deliberately false. `body.label` is `c68before`, echoed by the server.
  expect body.label equals "not-the-label-the-server-echoed"

  # Must never be reached. Under `check` semantics it would be, and `counts.marks` would carry
  # `c68after: 1` — which is the single number separating the two constructs.
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c68after" }

@constructs @workhorses
test "a `capture` that resolves to nothing fails the step rather than binding undefined"
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c69before" }
  expect status equals 200

  # `thereIsNoSuchField` is not in the payload — `mark` answers `{ label, count }` and nothing else.
  # The contract is that this **step** fails. The alternative implementation binds `undefined` and
  # says nothing, and every consequence of that shows up later, somewhere else, as a confusing
  # interpolation of the empty string.
  capture body.thereIsNoSuchField as missing

  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c69after" }

@constructs @workhorses
test "`capture` binds the value it read, not a live reference to the current response"
  # The passing half of `C69`, and the direction the negative above cannot reach. A `capture`
  # implemented as a lazy read against "the response in scope" would answer `c69second` on the last
  # line — every existing use in this repository would still be green, because none of them issues
  # another request between a capture and its use.
  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c69bound" }
  expect status equals 200
  capture body.label as first

  api POST /lifecycle/mark body { label: "c69second" }
  expect status equals 200

  expect {first} equals "c69bound"
